1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:08,600
Congratulations! You've passed a one percent treaty. Now you've got twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year flowing

2
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:14,600
into the one percent Treaty Fund. That's the good news. The bad news is that humans are involved.

3
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:22,820
Not all of it is available for allocation. First, the automatic deductions (think of these as the "cost of convincing humans

4
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:31,180
to stop killing each other" tax): Victory Incentive Alignment Bond investors receive ten percent, or two point seven two

5
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:39,400
billion dollars, to repay campaign funders. Political incentives, or I.A.B.s, receive ten percent, or two point seven two

6
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:47,560
billion dollars, to keep politicians aligned. The amount available for Wishocracy is eighty percent, or twenty-one point

7
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:54,820
eight billion dollars. How do you stop parasitic special interests from stealing that twenty-one point eight billion dollars?

8
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:03,220
If history is any guide, you don't. Every pool of money humanity has ever created has attracted lobbyists the way a dropped

9
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,360
ice cream cone attracts ants, except ants at least work hard.

10
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:12,260
Wishocracy replaces committees with code and representatives with mathematics.

11
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:19,840
It lets eight billion people collectively decide how to spend twenty-one point eight billion dollars without turning it

12
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:25,000
into a bureaucratic nightmare. The difference between this and the N.I.H.

13
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
is like asking two hundred bureaucrats what you should have for lunch versus asking your stomach.

14
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:35,600
Your stomach has never once funded a colleague's lunch out of professional courtesy.

15
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:40,740
How Wishocracy Allocates the one percent Treaty Fund: Decentralized Crowdfunding.

16
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:48,480
Your decentralized institutes of health, or D.I.H. network functions as a decentralized crowdfunding platform.

17
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:56,920
Anyone can submit a campaign proposal. Wishocracy allocates funds from the one percent Treaty Fund across competing campaigns.

18
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:01,680
It's Kickstarter, but backed by the G.D.P. of nations instead of your friend's credit card.

19
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:09,960
What your decentralized F.D.A. handles automatically (no voting required): Companies register and patients choose which

20
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:14,120
treatments get tested, which is capitalism, for once, doing something useful.

21
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,480
Patients join trials for their conditions to decide which diseases get researched, as people tend to be motivated about

22
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:27,380
their own mortality. Resource allocation within trials is handled by market prices and participant choices.

23
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:35,900
What Wishocracy Actually Decides: The hard stuff. Allocation across campaign proposals competing for one percent Treaty

24
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:40,500
Fund funds: Infrastructure Campaigns (the boring but essential plumbing).

25
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:47,860
The Decentralized Framework for Drug Assessment Development has a one billion dollar per year proposal.

26
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:48,320
The Epic electronic health records, or E.H.R. Integration Project, has a five million dollar one-time proposal.

27
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:52,840
A.W.S. Infrastructure Services has a three million dollar per year proposal.

28
00:02:53,420 --> 00:02:56,460
The Security Audit Program has a two million dollar per year proposal.

29
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:03,420
An alternative decentralized F.D.A. has an eight million dollar per year proposal (competition!

30
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:10,820
novel concept for government!). Public Goods (things the market won't fund because nobody gets rich curing diseases that

31
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:12,700
don't sell pills). Public goods are things the market won't fund because nobody gets rich curing diseases that don't sell

32
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:17,920
pills. Patient Trial Subsidies Program, eight hundred million dollars per year, by automatic formula.

33
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:25,720
Off-Patent Drug Research, twenty billion dollars per year. Rare Disease Initiative, ten billion dollars per year.

34
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:34,340
Negative Results Publishing Fund, five billion dollars per year (yes, knowing what *doesn't* work is worth billions; your

35
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:38,580
species keeps re-running failed experiments because nobody publishes failures).

36
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:46,960
Service Provider Bids (capitalism, but supervised). Data Storage Provider A, two billion dollars per year.

37
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:51,800
Data Storage Provider B, one point five billion dollars per year, competing.

38
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:57,480
Pragmatic Clinical Trial Insurance Pool, ten billion dollar reserve.

39
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:07,280
How It Works: Pairwise Comparisons Between Campaigns. No committee on Earth has ever looked at a list of ten thousand proposals

40
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:12,620
and made a good decision. Committees are where good ideas go to die of old age.

41
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:22,460
Instead, the global population votes using pairwise comparisons: "What's more important right now:" Framework for drug assessment

42
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:28,720
development versus Alzheimer's prize? Epic integration versus Security audits?

43
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:34,380
Rare disease research versus An alternative drug assessment framework?

44
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:41,720
Patient subsidies versus Infrastructure spending? Millions of people make simple pairwise choices.

45
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:45,960
The algorithm aggregates preferences into funding allocations.

46
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:53,640
No filibustering. No horse-trading. No senators holding medical research hostage because they want a highway in their district.

47
00:04:54,940 --> 00:05:01,000
Why You Can't Just Use a Spreadsheet. Someone is going to ask, "Why not just write a formula?"

48
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:06,780
Because the decisions look like this: One. Multiple competing implementations.

49
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:11,020
Three teams want to build the drug assessment platform. Which one?

50
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:17,740
All three? Two? The one with the best logo? Two. Trade-offs between prizes.

51
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:26,540
One hundred billion dollars for aging reversal or fifty billion dollars for Alzheimer's plus fifty billion dollars for cancer?

52
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:34,860
There is no "correct" answer. There is only what humanity collectively values, which is exactly what Wishocracy measures.

53
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:42,420
Three. Public goods allocation. How much for off-patent drugs versus rare diseases?

54
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:47,980
Markets won't answer this because there's no profit in it. Four.

55
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:52,640
Service provider competition. Which companies get contracts for what amounts?

56
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:58,780
Your decentralized F.D.A. allocates resources WITHIN pragmatic clinical trials.

57
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:05,900
Wishocracy allocates resources BETWEEN campaigns. One is the engine, the other is the steering wheel.

58
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:14,700
You need both unless you enjoy driving into walls. The core of Wishocracy is a voting method so simple, even a congressman

59
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:23,160
could do it (though we'd recommend they practice first). It's called Aggregated Pairwise Preference Allocation, or simply,

60
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:29,270
the Pairwise Slider Allocation. Your brain can't rank a list of twenty priorities.

61
00:06:29,820 --> 00:06:34,400
It gives up around item number seven. But it's fantastic at comparing two things.

62
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:43,120
Instead of asking, "Rank these ten thousand campaign proposals," the system asks: "What's more important right now: decentralized

63
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:48,640
F.D.A. protocol development or security audits?" You make a choice.

64
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:52,380
That's it. You do this a few times with different random pairs.

65
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:58,660
It takes five minutes. Less time than you spend choosing a Netflix show, and considerably more important.

66
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:05,500
Millions of other people do the same. An algorithm takes all these simple, head-to-head comparisons and builds a real-time

67
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:12,720
ranking of infrastructure and public goods priorities. (Remember: Wishocracy doesn't allocate between diseases.

68
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:20,000
Patients do that by choosing which trials to join. Wishocracy allocates between infrastructure campaigns and public goods

69
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,980
that markets can't handle.). It's the wisdom of crowds, but without the crowds having to talk to each other.

70
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:34,300
Why This Actually Works (Math Warning). Random pairs prevent gaming, because you cannot lobby five million people, even

71
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:43,060
if you tried. Statistical models (Bradley-Terry, PageRank) extract global preferences from individual comparisons, using

72
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:48,660
the same math Google uses to find cat videos to instead help you cure cancer.

73
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:56,520
Outliers cancel out because the person who votes to fund a personal jetpack gets drowned out by the millions voting to cure

74
00:07:56,520 --> 00:08:02,900
their mother's Alzheimer's. The wisdom of crowds emerges reliably, repeatedly, and boringly.

75
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:11,000
Example with real numbers: Five million people vote, with each person making twenty comparisons to create one hundred million

76
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:19,480
data points. The algorithm processes them in seconds. The output is: "Allocate forty percent to patient subsidy programs,

77
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:27,680
twenty-five percent to decentralized F.D.A. platform development, fifteen percent to off-patent drug research, ten percent

78
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:35,679
to rare disease initiatives, five percent to security infrastructure, and five percent to negative results publishing."

79
00:08:35,679 --> 00:08:38,280
No committee meetings were harmed in the making of this budget.

80
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:45,820
It's democracy without the shouting. The stupidity is still there, but it's evenly distributed across millions of people

81
00:08:45,820 --> 00:08:50,420
and thus cancels out. This is called "the wisdom of crowds."

82
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:57,180
It works for the same reason that guessing the weight of a cow at a fair works: individually you're all terrible at it,

83
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:04,400
but collectively you're weirdly accurate. From Priorities to Projects (Where Wishes Become Tasks).

84
00:09:05,380 --> 00:09:11,060
Great, so the Pairwise Slider Allocation tells you "Curing Alzheimer's" is a top priority.

85
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:16,240
Wonderful. Now what? Having a priority without a plan is just a wish.

86
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:22,880
And wishes, as a rule, don't cure anything. Wishocracy translates that priority into action.

87
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:32,720
AI Breaks It Down: An A.I. takes the impossible goal of "Cure Alzheimer's" and breaks it into thousands of smaller, concrete,

88
00:09:33,160 --> 00:09:37,940
fundable tasks. "Cure Alzheimer's" becomes "Map protein structures."

89
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:44,780
That becomes "Run AlphaFold on these sequences." That becomes "Rent computing time."

90
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:51,020
Every impossible problem is just a series of possible steps arranged in a line.

91
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:58,080
Your species figured this out for building pyramids four thousand five hundred years ago but keeps forgetting to apply it

92
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:04,100
to medicine. The Bounty Board: The system posts these tasks to a global marketplace.

93
00:10:04,660 --> 00:10:09,500
It's like eBay, but for saving humanity. WANTED: A cure for Alzheimer's.

94
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:16,480
BOUNTY: ten billion dollars. WANTED: A map of all protein misfolding patterns.

95
00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:24,100
BOUNTY: five hundred million dollars. The World Competes: The best teams from around the world, from M.I.T.

96
00:10:24,380 --> 00:10:30,740
to some kid in a garage in Lagos, bid on these tasks. The system funds multiple approaches in parallel.

97
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:36,100
The ones that show promise get more funding. The ones that fail lose funding instantly.

98
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:44,680
It's venture capital, but for not dying. Your species already uses this method to decide which restaurants survive and which

99
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:48,780
phone apps get downloaded. Time to try it with the thing that kills you.
